<p>&#8220;A small programme with a large international reach has bucked the trend of closures across the arts and humanities.&#8221;<span id="more-58225"></span></p>
<p>Over the summer, administration of the University of Dundee announced a plan to cut costs by, among other things, no longer offering students the option of majoring in philosophy. A campaign was launched to save the philosophy program there, and a petition in support of it garnered close to 6000 signatories.</p>
<p>As a result of the petition, statements from academic organizations, and the efforts of faculty and students, <a href="https://www.dundee.ac.uk/philosophy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">philosophy at Dundee</a> will continue, as the <a href="https://www.dundee.ac.uk/humanities/people?f%5B0%5D=group%3A846" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Philosophy Team</a> at Dundee explains in the following guest post.</p>
<p>Thanks to <a href="https://www.dundee.ac.uk/people/oisin-keohane">Oisin Keohane</a> for keeping us informed.</p>
